\pard\sa120\keepn\b\fs34\cf2 Types of Data (cont.)\b0\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Most business applications primarily use String, Decimal, Single, and Integer data types. Here are examples of data that may be stored to computer memory:\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Customer Name \u8211? String \u8211? Stores alphabetic characters.\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Social Security Number \u8211? String \u8211? Numbers that are not used in computations.\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Customer Number \u8211? String \u8211? Numbers that are not used in computations.\par
\pard\sb520\sa40\keepn\f0\fs18\cf5 SLIDE 10\par
\pard\sa120\keepn\b\fs34\cf2 Types of Data (cont.)\b0\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Balance Due \u8211? Decimal \u8211? Used in calculations and often requires a decimal point to separate dollars and cents.\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Quantity in Inventory \u8211? Integer or Short \u8211? Selection depends on how large the quantity in inventory will be, but the quantity is usually a whole number.\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Sales Tax Rate \u8211? Single or Decimal \u8211? Used to store a percentage value; also used for scientific calculations.\par
\pard\sb520\sa40\keepn\f0\fs18\cf5 SLIDE 11\par
\pard\sa120\keepn\b\fs34\cf2 Naming Rules for Variables and Constants\b0\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab You as the programmer decide what to name variables and constants \u8211? there are technical rules you must follow when creating variable and constant names.\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Names can include letters, digits, and the underscore, but must begin with a letter.\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Names cannot contain spaces or periods.\par
\pard\sb520\sa40\keepn\f0\fs18\cf5 SLIDE 12\par
\pard\sa120\keepn\b\fs34\cf2 Naming Rules for Variables and Constants (cont.)\b0\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Names cannot be VB reserved words such as LET, PRINT, DIM, or CONST.\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Names are not case sensitive.  This means that TotalInteger, TOTALINTEGER, and totalinteger are all equivalent names.\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab For all intensive purposes, a Name can be as long as you want (the actual limit is 16,383 characters in length).\par
\pard\sb520\sa40\keepn\f0\fs18\cf5 SLIDE 13\par
\pard\sa120\keepn\b\fs34\cf2 Declaring Variables\b0\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Declare variables that are local with the Dim statement.  Declare variables that are module-level with the Private statement.\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Dim statement \u8211? use this to declare variables and constants inside a procedure these are local variables.\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab The Dim statement needs to specify a variable/constant name and data type.\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Specifying an initial value for a variable is optional.\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Constants must have an initial value specified.\par
\pard\sb520\sa40\keepn\f0\fs18\cf5 SLIDE 14\par
\pard\sa120\keepn\b\fs34\cf2 Examples:\b0\par
\pard\li360\sb60\sa140\f1\fs22\cf5 {\cf2 Dim}{\cf5  }{\cf5 StudentNameString}{\cf5  }{\cf2 As}{\cf5  }{\cf2 String}\line {\cf2 Dim}{\cf5  }{\cf5 CountStudentsInteger}{\cf5  }{\cf2 As}{\cf5  }{\cf2 Integer}\line {\cf2 Dim}{\cf5  }{\cf5 AccountBalanceDecimal}{\cf5  }{\cf2 As}{\cf5  }{\cf2 Decimal}{\cf5  }{\cf5 =}{\cf5  }{\cf6 100D}\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab You can also declare more than one variable in a Dim statement.\par
\pard\li360\sb60\sa140\f1\fs22\cf5 {\cf2 Dim}{\cf5  }{\cf5 StudentNameString}{\cf5 ,}{\cf5  }{\cf5 MajorString}{\cf5  }{\cf2 As}{\cf5  }{\cf2 String}\line {\cf2 Dim}{\cf5  }{\cf5 SubtotalDecimal}{\cf5 ,}{\cf5  }{\cf5 TotalDecimal}{\cf5 ,}{\cf5  }{\cf5 TaxAmountDueDecimal}{\cf5  }{\cf2 As}{\cf5  }{\cf2 Decimal}\par
\pard\sb520\sa40\keepn\f0\fs18\cf5 SLIDE 15\par
\pard\sa120\keepn\b\fs34\cf2 Declaring Constants\b0\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Declare constants with the Const statement.\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Constants are similar to variables \u8211? constants are values that are stored to memory locations; however, a constant cannot have its value change during program execution \u8211? constant values are generally fixed over time.\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Examples:  sales tax rate or name of one of the states in the United States.\par
\pard\sb520\sa40\keepn\f0\fs18\cf5 SLIDE 16\par
\pard\sa120\keepn\b\fs34\cf2 Declaring Constants (cont.)\b0\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab VB has two different types of constants.\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Intrinsic Constants \u8211? these are defined as enumerations such as Color.Red and Color.Blue.  These are called intrinsic because they are predefined in VB and always exist for your use.\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Named Constants \u8211? these are constants you define with a Const statement.  These constants are specific to your programming application.\par
\pard\sb520\sa40\keepn\f0\fs18\cf5 SLIDE 17\par
\pard\sa120\keepn\b\fs34\cf2 Declaring Constants (cont.)\b0\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Examples:\par
\pard\li360\sb60\sa140\f1\fs22\cf5 {\cf2 Const}{\cf5  }{\cf5 SALES_TAX_RATE_SINGLE}{\cf5  }{\cf2 As}{\cf5  }{\cf2 Single}{\cf5  }{\cf5 =}{\cf5  }{\cf6 0.0725F}\line {\cf2 Const}{\cf5  }{\cf5 BIG_STATE_NAME_STRING}{\cf5  }{\cf2 As}{\cf5  }{\cf2 String}{\cf5  }{\cf5 =}{\cf5  }{\cf3 "Alaska"}\line {\cf2 Const}{\cf5  }{\cf5 TITLE_STRING}{\cf5  }{\cf2 As}{\cf5  }{\cf2 String}{\cf5  }{\cf5 =}{\cf5  }{\cf3 "Data Entry Error"}\line {\cf2 Const}{\cf5  }{\cf5 MAX_SIZE_INTEGER}{\cf5  }{\cf2 As}{\cf5  }{\cf2 Integer}{\cf5  }{\cf5 =}{\cf5  }{\cf6 4000}\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab String (text or character) constants are assigned values within the " " (double quote) marks.  This statement stores double-quote marks as part of the string \u8211? do this by typing two double-quote marks together.\par
\pard\sb520\sa40\keepn\f0\fs18\cf5 SLIDE 18\par
\pard\sa120\keepn\b\fs34\cf2 Declaring Constants (cont.)\b0\par
\pard\li360\sb60\sa140\f1\fs22\cf5 {\cf2 Const}{\cf5  }{\cf5 COURSE_TITLE_STRING}{\cf5  }{\cf2 As}{\cf5  }{\cf2 String}{\cf5  }{\cf5 =}{\cf5  }{\cf3 "Programming Visual Basic"}\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Numeric constants like those shown above do NOT use double-quote marks \u8211? just type the numeric value numbers.  Follow these rules for assigning numeric values to constants:\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab You can use numbers, a decimal point, and a plus (+) or minus (-) sign.\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Do not include special characters such as a comma, dollar sign, or other special characters.\par
\pard\sb520\sa40\keepn\f0\fs18\cf5 SLIDE 19\par
\pard\sa120\keepn\b\fs34\cf2 Declaring Constants (cont.)\b0\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Append one of these characters to the end of the numeric constant or variable to denote a data type declaration.  If you do not use these, a whole number is assumed to be Integer and a fractional value is assumed to be Double.\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Decimal         D    40.45D\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Double         R    12576.877R\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Integer         I    47852I\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Long             L    9888444222L\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Short             S    2588S\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Single         F    0.0725F\par
\pard\sb520\sa40\keepn\f0\fs18\cf5 SLIDE 20\par
\pard\sa120\keepn\b\fs34\cf2 Scope of Variables and Constants\b0\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Each variable (or constant) has a finite lifetime and visibility \u8211? termed the Scope. The variable lifetime is how long the variable exists before the computer operating system garbage-collects the memory allocated to the stored value.\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab There are four levels of scope.\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Namespace (use a Public Shared declaration instead of Dim) \u8211? the variable is visible within the entire project (applicable to a project with multiple forms).\par
\pard\sb520\sa40\keepn\f0\fs18\cf5 SLIDE 21\par
\pard\sa120\keepn\b\fs34\cf2 Scope of Variables and Constants (cont.)\b0\par
\pard\tx720\li720\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8211?\tab The variable is project-wide and can be used in any procedure in any form in the project.\par
\pard\tx720\li720\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8211?\tab The variable memory allocation is garbage-collected when application execution terminates.\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Modulelevel (usually use Private to declare the variable; use Const to declare the constant) \u8211? a variable/constant can be used in any procedure on a specific form \u8211? it is not visible to other Forms.\par
\pard\tx720\li720\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8211?\tab Use module-level variables when the values that are stored in their memory locations are used in more than one procedure (click event or other type of procedure).\par
\pard\sb520\sa40\keepn\f0\fs18\cf5 SLIDE 22\par
\pard\sa120\keepn\b\fs34\cf2 Scope of Variables and Constants (cont.)\b0\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab A module-level variable or constant is created (allocated memory) when a form loads into memory and the variable or constant remains in memory until the form is unloaded.\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Local (use Dim to declare the variable; use Const to declare the constant) \u8211? a variable/constant is declared and used only within a single procedure.\par
\pard\tx720\li720\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8211?\tab Variable lifetime is the period for which a variable exists.\par
\pard\tx720\li720\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8211?\tab When a procedure executes, such as when you click on a button control, each variable and constant declared as local within the procedure executes, "uncreated" when the procedure executes the End Sub statement.\par
\pard\tx720\li720\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8211?\tab Each time you click the button, a new set of variables and constants are created.\par
\pard\sb520\sa40\keepn\f0\fs18\cf5 SLIDE 23\par
\pard\sa120\keepn\b\fs34\cf2 Scope of Variables and Constants (cont.)\b0\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Block (use Dim to declare the variable; use Const to declare the constant) \u8211? the variable/constant is only visible within a small portion of a procedure \u8211? these variables/constants are rarely created.\par
\pard\sb520\sa40\keepn\f0\fs18\cf5 SLIDE 24\par
\pard\sa120\keepn\b\fs34\cf2 Scope of Variables and Constants (cont.)\b0\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab This figure illustrates where to declare local versus module-level variables/constants.  Later you will learn to declare namespace and block variables and constants and when to use the Public keyword in place of Private.\par
\pard\sb520\sa40\keepn\f0\fs18\cf5 SLIDE 25\par
