\pard\sa120\keepn\b\fs34\cf2 Converting Output Data Types (cont.)\b0\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Implicit Conversion \u8211? this is conversion by VB from a narrower to wider data type (less memory to more memory) \u8211? this is done automatically as there is no danger of losing any precision.  In this example, an integer (4 bytes) is converted to a double (8 bytes):\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab BiggerNumberDouble = SmallerNumberInteger\par
\pard\sb520\sa40\keepn\f0\fs18\cf5 SLIDE 29\par
\pard\sa120\keepn\b\fs34\cf2 Converting Output Data Types (cont.)\b0\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Explicit Conversion \u8211? this is also called Casting and is used to convert between numeric data types that do not support implicit conversion.  This table shows use of the Convert method to convert one numeric data type to another numeric data type.  Note that fractional values are rounded when converting to integer.\par
