\pard\sa120\keepn\b\fs34\cf2 Converting Output Data Types (cont.)\b0\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Use the Parse method to convert a string to a number Performing Calculations \u8211? VB uses a wider data type when calculations include unlike data types or to parse the value in a textbox control.\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab This example produces a decimal result.\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab AverageSaleDecimal = TotalSalesDecimal / CountInteger\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Summary Rules:\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Use the Convert method to convert a type of number to a different type of number.\par
\pard\sb520\sa40\keepn\f0\fs18\cf5 SLIDE 32\par
\pard\sa120\keepn\b\fs34\cf2 Arithmetic Operators\b0\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab The arithmetic operators are the same as in many other programming languages. They are:\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab +  Addition\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Subtraction\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab * Multiplication\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab /   Division\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab ^   Exponentiation\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab \\   Integer Division\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Mod  Modulus Division\par
\pard\sb520\sa40\keepn\f0\fs18\cf5 SLIDE 33\par
\pard\sa120\keepn\b\fs34\cf2 Arithmetic Operators (cont.)\b0\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Exponentiation \u8211? This raises a number to the specified power \u8211? the result produced is data type Double.  Example:\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab ValueSquaredDouble = NumberDecimal ^ 2\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab ValueCubedDouble = NumberDecimal ^ 3\par
\pard\sb520\sa40\keepn\f0\fs18\cf5 SLIDE 34\par
\pard\sa120\keepn\b\fs34\cf2 Arithmetic Operators (cont.)\b0\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Integer Division \u8211? Divide one integer by another leaving an integer result and discarding the remainder, if any.  Example:\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab If the variable MinutesInteger= 130, then this expression returns the value of 2 hours.\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab HoursInteger = MinutesInteger \\ 60\par
\pard\sb520\sa40\keepn\f0\fs18\cf5 SLIDE 35\par
\pard\sa120\keepn\b\fs34\cf2 Arithmetic Operators (cont.)\b0\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Modulus Division \u8211? This returns the remainder of a division operation.  Using the same value for MinutesInteger= 500, this expression returns the value 20 minutes and can be used to calculate the amount of overtime worked for an 8-hour work day.\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab MinutesInteger = MinutesInteger Mod 60\par
\pard\sb520\sa40\keepn\f0\fs18\cf5 SLIDE 36\par
\pard\sa120\keepn\b\fs34\cf2 Order of Precedence\b0\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab The order of precedence for expressions that have more than one operation is the same as for other programming languages.\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab Evaluate values and calculation symbols in this order:\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab (1) Values enclosed inside parentheses\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab (2) Exponentiation\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab (3) Multiplication and Division\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab (4) Integer Division\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab (5) Modulus Division\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab (6) Addition and Subtraction\par
\pard\sb520\sa40\keepn\f0\fs18\cf5 SLIDE 37\par
\pard\sa120\keepn\b\fs34\cf2 Assignment Operators and Formulas\b0\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab The equal sign is the assignment operator.  It means store the value of the expression on the right side of the equal sign to the memory variable named on the left side of the equal sign.  Examples:\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab ItemValueDecimal = QuantityInteger * PriceDecimal\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab HoursWorkedSingle = MinutesWorkedSingle / 60F\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab NetProfitDecimal = GrossSalesDecimal \u8211? CostGoodsSoldDecimal\par
\pard\sb520\sa40\keepn\f0\fs18\cf5 SLIDE 38\par
\pard\sa120\keepn\b\fs34\cf2 Assignment Operators and Formulas (cont.)\b0\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab The plus symbol combined with the equal sign allows you to accumulate a value in a memory variable.  Examples\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab TotalSalesDecimal += SaleAmountDecimal\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab is equivalent to the following \u8211? it means take the current value of TotalSalesDecimaland add to it the value of SaleAmountDecimal and store it back to TotalSalesDecimal(it gets bigger and BIGGER).\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab TotalSalesDecimal = TotalSalesDecimal + SaleAmountDecimal\par
\pard\sb520\sa40\keepn\f0\fs18\cf5 SLIDE 39\par
\pard\sa120\keepn\b\fs34\cf2 Assignment Operators and Formulas (cont.)\b0\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab The minus symbol combined with the equal sign allows you to decrement or count backwards.  Examples:\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab CountInteger -= 1\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab is equivalent to\par
\pard\tx360\li360\fi-360\sa80\f0\fs26\cf1 \u8226?\tab CountInteger = CountInteger - 1\par
\pard\sb400\f0\fs20\cf5 \u8212? End of Lesson 5 \u8212?\par
